Q: Is it possible in HTML5 to give translation service a hint for translating a specific element A: <p>You can add <strong>invisible and not selectable/copyable</strong> text that provides context such that the translation is correct. For example, instead of &quot;train&quot;, you can write &quot;railway: train&quot; or &quot;sports training: train&quot; but make only the text &quot;train&quot; visible.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
.hint-for-translation {
  zoom: 0.01;
  font-size: 0.01px;
  user-select: none;
}
font {
  /* Hide the space character that Google Page Translate puts in
  the &lt;font&gt; elements that it adds. You can change this rule depending
  on what looks best with your page layout. */
  display: inline-block;
}
&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="hint-for-translation"&gt;railway: &lt;/span&gt;train&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="hint-for-translation"&gt;sports training: &lt;/span&gt;train&lt;/div&gt;

Warning: Chrome currently doesn't translate outputs of Stack Overflow snippets.
You have to save the code to an HTML file.</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>To keep track of whether Google Translate keeps inserting <code>&lt;font&gt;</code>: <a href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41407169" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41407169</a></p>
<p>Alternative variants:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If you <strong>omit the colon &quot;:&quot;</strong>, the hint might get translated as an adjective of the main text, and in languages with a different word order, the translatin of the main text gets pulled into the front of the hint <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> (due to a bug in the translation software) and thus becomes invisible. So don't omit the colon.</p>
<ul>
<li>If you don't mind displaying the hints after translation and only want to hide them before translation, you can avoid said problem. Chrome translation adds <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> elements, so you can:
<pre><code>.hint-for-translation:has(font) {
  zoom: revert;
  font-size: revert;
}
</code></pre>
(Related: <code>inherit</code>, <code>initial</code>.)
But it's risky because it relies on them continuing to use <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> like that.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Similarly, the translator translates <strong>hashtags</strong> not as hashtags but as adjectives into some languages, leading to the same problem.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Putting hints into <strong>brackets</strong> worked in my tests, but I expect similar risks for some other text.</p>
</li>
<li><p>So using <strong>a colon &quot;:&quot; seems best</strong> so far.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Brave Translate</strong> has a bug: it seems to translate each text node separately, ignoring context from elements like <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;em&gt;</code>. Assigning which word got translated approximately to which word might require a big rework of their software. In the meantime, you can have a <code>.hint-for-translation</code> element saying &quot;railway:&quot;, another one saying &quot;railway: train&quot;, and a visible element saying &quot;train&quot;, use JS to watch for translations as in another answer here, and set the content of the visible element to the <strong>translation of &quot;railway: train&quot; <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10398931/how-to-remove-text-from-a-string">minus</a> that of &quot;railway:&quot;</strong>. Or remove everything before the colon from the translation of &quot;railway: train&quot; if you can take into consideration how the colon gets translated into all relevant languages, for example <code>：</code> (U+FF1A Fullwidth Colon, as opposed to <code>:</code>).</p>

Q: Angular/Supabase project will not load upon &quot;ng serve,&quot; causing infinite rendering A: <p>Have you fixed that? For me run outside angualr workis</p>
<pre><code>import { inject, Injectable, NgZone, OnDestroy, signal, InjectionToken } from '@angular/core';
import {
  AuthChangeEvent,
  createClient,
  Provider,
  Session,
  SupabaseClient as SupabaseClientType,
} from '@supabase/supabase-js';

export interface SupabaseConfig {
  supabaseUrl: string;
  supabaseKey: string;
}

export const SUPABASE_CONFIG = new InjectionToken&lt;SupabaseConfig&gt;('SUPABASE_CONFIG');

@Injectable({ providedIn: 'root' })
export class SupabaseClient implements OnDestroy {
  session: Session | null = null;
  readonly sessionSig = signal&lt;Session | null&gt;(null);
  readonly ready = signal(false);

  private supabase: SupabaseClientType;
  private sub?: { data: { subscription: { unsubscribe(): void } } };
  private readonly ngZone = inject(NgZone);
  private readonly config = inject(SUPABASE_CONFIG);

  constructor() {
    this.supabase = this.ngZone.runOutsideAngular(() =&gt;
      createClient(this.config.supabaseUrl, this.config.supabaseKey)
    );
    this.initializeSession();
  }

  ngOnDestroy() {
    this.sub?.data.subscription.unsubscribe();
  }

  getSession(): Session | null {
    return this.sessionSig();
  }

  async getCurrentSession(): Promise&lt;Session | null&gt; {
    const s = this.sessionSig();
    if (s) return s;
    const {
      data: { session },
    } = await this.supabase.auth.getSession();
    this.sessionSig.set(session);
    return session;
  }

  authChanges(cb: (event: AuthChangeEvent, session: Session | null) =&gt; void) {
    return this.supabase.auth.onAuthStateChange(cb);
  }

  get getClient() {
    return this.supabase;
  }

  signInWithEmail(email: string) {
    return this.supabase.auth.signInWithOtp({ email });
  }
  signInWithPassword(email: string, password: string) {
    return this.supabase.auth.signInWithPassword({ email, password });
  }
  signUp(email: string, password: string) {
    return this.supabase.auth.signUp({ email, password });
  }
  signInWithProvider(provider: Provider) {
    return this.supabase.auth.signInWithOAuth({
      provider,
      options: { redirectTo: window.location.origin },
    });
  }
  signOut() {
    return this.supabase.auth.signOut();
  }

  private async initializeSession(): Promise&lt;void&gt; {
    const {
      data: { session },
    } = await this.supabase.auth.getSession();
    this.ngZone.run(() =&gt; {
      this.sessionSig.set(session);
      this.ready.set(true);
    });

    this.sub = this.supabase.auth.onAuthStateChange((event, session) =&gt; {
      this.ngZone.run(() =&gt; this.sessionSig.set(session));
    });
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Graph API Unable to read JSON request payload A: <p>Like @Optimal already mentioned. It is good to escape the quotation marks of the HTML Body value.</p>
<p>However, I see the request body also has a <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/graph/api/user-sendmail?view=graph-rest-1.0&amp;tabs=http#request-body" rel="nofollow noreferrer">saveToSentItems boolean</a> property. Can you try it with that boolean as well?</p>
<p>Try something like below:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;message&quot;: {
    &quot;subject&quot;: &quot;@{triggerBody()['text']}&quot;,
    &quot;body&quot;: {
      &quot;contentType&quot;: &quot;HTML&quot;,
      &quot;content&quot;: &quot;@{triggerBody()['text_1']}&quot;
    },
    &quot;toRecipients&quot;: [
      {
        &quot;emailAddress&quot;: {
          &quot;address&quot;: &quot;@{triggerBody()['text_2']}&quot;
        }
      }
    ]
    },
  &quot;saveToSentItems&quot;: &quot;true&quot;
}
</code></pre>
